~ Console output lives in the core.io module (imported explicitly).
~   print(x)   writes x + a newline to stdout (any renderable value)
~   eprint(x)  same, to stderr
~   write(content, fd)  the rendered bytes to a file descriptor; returns the byte count
~ `print(x)` is the ergonomic form of `write(x, stdout)` plus the newline. On output,
~ `print` shows its value for a reader; `write` is byte-exact. Both measure a Text in bytes.
~ `<< core.test` verifies the byte count `write` returns; on success the program exits 0.
<< core.io
<< core.test

~ The built-in claims `write` at two arguments, leaving every other arity free: this ADDS a
~ one-argument `write` member, and the built-in it calls still reaches the runtime. Every
~ call picks the member its argument types match.
write = (content :: Text) -> Num => write(content, stdout)

^ = () -> $ => <
  print("hello")            ~ stdout: hello\n
  written :: Num = write("raw", stdout)   ~ stdout: raw   (no newline)
  assert(written, equals(3))      ~ "raw" is 3 bytes
  assert(write("!"), equals(1))   ~ the one-argument member, picked by its type

  ~ What reaches the descriptor is bytes, not characters: "héllo" is five characters and
  ~ six bytes, and `print` writes the same six plus a newline.
  assert(write("héllo"), equals(6))
  print("héllo")            ~ stdout: héllo\n

  eprint("done")            ~ stderr: done\n
>
